{
  /*
    The serverless functions were in no tsconfig at all.

    tsconfig.json is a solution file with "files": [] and two references, so
    `tsc --noEmit -p .` checked nothing and reported success. tsconfig.app
    includes only src, tsconfig.node only vite.config.ts. Everything under
    netlify/functions therefore went unchecked from the day it was written,
    which is the half of this codebase that takes money, writes tags and books
    calendars.

    It cost real money. verify-payment called scheduleSecondInstalment with
    three arguments where it takes four, so the session id was undefined and
    every second instalment was raised under the idempotency key
    "install-2-undefined-invoice". Stripe returns the original object for a
    repeated key rather than creating a new one, so the first client to pay got
    an invoice and every client after them silently got nothing. A one line
    arity error, invisible because nothing was looking.
  */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    /* DOM is here for its fetch types, whose json() resolves to any. Node's
       undici types resolve it to unknown, which turns every handler's
       res.json().something into an error and buries the real findings. */
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "types": ["node"],

    /*
      Deliberately not strict, and that is a considered trade rather than
      laziness. Turning strict on here produces about fifty errors, nearly all
      of one harmless shape: res.json() is typed unknown, and every handler
      reads a property straight off it. None of those are bugs, the runtime
      code copes, and a check that reports fifty things nobody will act on is a
      check nobody runs.

      Arity and argument type errors, which is the class that just cost a
      $5,000 invoice per client, are reported with strict off. So this catches
      what matters today and stays green, which means it will actually be run.
      Tighten it later by fixing the json shapes, not by flipping this and
      living with a red build.
    */
    "strict": false,
    "noUnusedLocals": false,
    "noUnusedParameters": false,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,

    "allowImportingTsExtensions": false,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["netlify/functions"]
}
